{
  "id": "74uinrh",
  "title": "CHAPTER 3: GEOGRAPHY OF ANDHRA PRADESH",
  "order": 4,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Andhra Pradesh presents a diverse geographical landscape encompassing coastal plains, river deltas, plateaus, and hill ranges. The state's geography has shaped its agriculture, economy, culture, and settlement patterns.",
      "id": "wuv9z1g"
    },
    {
      "id": "70at5dp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.1 Physical Features"
    },
    {
      "id": "q8m11k5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3.1.1 Location and Extent"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Andhra Pradesh lies between 12°41' N to 19°07' N latitude and 76°46' E to 84°46' E longitude. It is bounded by the Bay of Bengal in the east, Tamil Nadu in the south, Karnataka in the west, and Telangana and Odisha in the north. The state spans approximately 700 km from north to south and 300-500 km from east to west.",
      "id": "4l98ald"
    },
    {
      "id": "tbxu5ls",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "3.1.2 Physiographic Divisions"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The state can be divided into three major physiographic regions:",
      "id": "qsrywim"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<strong>(a) The Eastern Ghats and Hill Ranges</strong>",
      "id": "6vow4qt"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The Eastern Ghats run through the state in a northeast-southwest direction, forming a discontinuous chain of hills. Major hill ranges include the Nallamala Hills (in Kurnool and Prakasam districts), the Erramalai Hills, the Velikonda Range, the Palakonda Range, and the Anantagiri Hills near Araku Valley. The highest peak in Andhra Pradesh is Arma Konda (also called Sitamma Konda) at 1680 m in Visakhapatnam district, in the Nallamala-Eastern Ghat range.",
      "id": "t9cmsbl"
    },
    {
      "id": "cssziog",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "8ju6d76",
      "src": "/books/Guide/Andhra_Pradesh CONSTABLE/images/image_8ju6d76.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "<strong>(b) The Deccan Plateau</strong> ",
      "id": "4z5asnv"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The western and southwestern parts of Andhra Pradesh form part of the Deccan Plateau, including the Rayalaseema region (comprising Kurnool, Kadapa, Anantapur, and Chittoor districts). This region is relatively arid with poor soils. Anantapur district receives the lowest rainfall in the state. The plateau is drained by tributaries of the Krishna River.",
      "id": "f1qswcg"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "<strong>(c) The Coastal Plains</strong>",
      "id": "uvlprzq"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "The coastal plains along the Bay of Bengal constitute the most fertile and densely populated part of Andhra Pradesh. The plains are wider in the delta regions of the Krishna and Godavari rivers (collectively called the 'Rice Bowl of India'). The coast includes beaches, estuaries, lagoons, and mangrove forests.",
      "id": "uekw26v"
    },
    {
      "id": "n56dlxp",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "xjodvz8",
      "src": "/books/Guide/Andhra_Pradesh CONSTABLE/images/image_xjodvz8.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "aguba7g",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3.2 Rivers"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Andhra Pradesh is well-watered by several major rivers. The two principal rivers are the Godavari and the Krishna.",
      "id": "25sswd0"
    },
    {
      "id": "dlifyom",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>River</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Length in AP (approx.)</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Features</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Godavari</p>",
            "<p>~480 km</p>",
            "<p>Longest river in peninsular India; originates in Trimbakeshwar, Maharashtra; called 'Dakshina Ganga'; drains into Bay of Bengal through large delta at Rajamahendravaram</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Krishna</p>",
            "<p>~480 km</p>",
            "<p>Originates in Mahabaleshwar, Maharashtra; second-largest river in peninsular India; Nagarjunasagar Dam is on this river; drains into Bay of Bengal near Machilipatnam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tungabhadra</p>",
            "<p>~300 km</p>",
            "<p>Tributary of Krishna; Srisailam and Tungabhadra dams; forms part of border with Karnataka</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Vamsadhara</p>",
            "<p>~250 km</p>",
            "<p>Flows through Srikakulam district; Vamsadhara Project</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Nagavali</p>",
            "<p>~165 km</p>",
            "<p>Flows through Vizianagaram and Srikakulam; drains into Bay of Bengal near Srikakulam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pennar (Penneru)</p>",
            "<p>~355 km (in AP)</p>",
            "<p>Originates in Chikkaballapur, Karnataka; drains into Bay of Bengal near Nellore</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Papagni</p>",
            "<p>~250 km</p>",
            "<p>Tributary of Pennar; originates in Andhra Pradesh</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sarada (Sarda)</p>",
            "<p>~170 km</p>",
            "<p>Flows through Visakhapatnam district; also called Eleru</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cheyyeru (Gundlakamma)</p>",
            "<p>~160 km</p>",
            "<p>Flows through Prakasam; seasonal river</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Gosthani</p>",
            "<p>~100 km</p>",
            "<p>Flows through Visakhapatnam and Bheemunipatnam area</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
